From: PhotoCraft AROS port
Subject: [PATCH] pthread: free the slot of a thread detached after it finished

Repository: deadwood2/AROS (ABIv11) 5376f09bc1ed0c36a12fbb096406013f8a046e89,
  compiler/pthread. The unmodified tree, built with -O2 -mcmodel=large
  -fno-omit-frame-pointer, reproduces all 33 objects of the older ABIv11
  SDK's lib/libpthread.a instruction for instruction, relocations included.
Date: 2026-10-16
Problem: pthread_detach() on a thread that has already returned only set
  `detached`. The thread had taken the joinable path (finished = TRUE, signal
  the waiter) and nothing frees the slot afterwards; its task pointer stays
  set although the task is gone. __pthread_Exit_Func then loops on
  `while (inf->task) Delay(1)` for that detached slot forever: the program
  cannot exit. Rust's std detaches every thread whose JoinHandle is dropped,
  so a short-lived worker that ends before its handle is dropped (common)
  wedges the exit. Seen with PhotoCraft on pool v11-2 (older AROS One 1.3):
  the main task sat in __pthread_Exit_Func waiting on SIGF_SINGLE (Delay),
  with no pthread task left alive.
Rationale: pthread_detach frees a finished thread's slot as pthread_join
  would; the thread's own end decides detached/finished under Forbid() so the
  two sides cannot both skip the cleanup.
Scope: two functions; behaviour changes only for detach-after-finish.
Upstream-status: mainline already has an equivalent fix:
  aros-development-team/AROS c31d3294fb1165642f6ff85028396ad4c3f80f12
  (2026-10-07, "pthread: end remaining threads at exit, fix detach/join
  races"; it also cancels threads still running at exit). deadwood2/AROS's
  default branch still had the old pthread_detach on 2026-10-16. Candidate
  for deadwood2/AROS as a backport of that commit (not submitted).
Prerequisites: none.
Verification: see the port's v11 run evidence (exit after the interactive
  session with the patched library linked privately).
Removal: when the system pthread link library has the fix.
---
diff --git a/compiler/pthread/pthread.c b/compiler/pthread/pthread.c
index 11f4e00..e29c468 100644
--- a/compiler/pthread/pthread.c
+++ b/compiler/pthread/pthread.c
@@ -914,7 +914,21 @@ int pthread_detach(pthread_t thread)
     if (inf->detached)
         return EINVAL;
 
+    Forbid();
+    if (inf->finished)
+    {
+        // The thread has already ended and nobody will join it: free the slot
+        // now, as pthread_join() would. Marking it detached instead left a slot
+        // whose task pointer never cleared, and the exit handler waited for
+        // that task forever.
+        Permit();
+        ObtainSemaphore(&thread_sem);
+        memset(inf, 0, sizeof(ThreadInfo));
+        ReleaseSemaphore(&thread_sem);
+        return 0;
+    }
     inf->detached = TRUE;
+    Permit();
 
     return 0;
 }
diff --git a/compiler/pthread/pthread_create.c b/compiler/pthread/pthread_create.c
index 5528138..92e2f1d 100644
--- a/compiler/pthread/pthread_create.c
+++ b/compiler/pthread/pthread_create.c
@@ -130,15 +130,20 @@ static void StarterFunc(void)
     }
     ReleaseSemaphore(&tls_sem);
 
+    // Decide under Forbid(): pthread_detach() looks at `finished` under Forbid()
+    // too, so exactly one side frees a slot that is detached after its thread
+    // has finished.
+    Forbid();
     if (!inf->detached)
     {
-        // tell the parent thread that we are done
-        Forbid();
+        // tell the parent thread that we are done; Forbid() lasts until this
+        // task has left the program's code
         inf->finished = TRUE;
         Signal(inf->waiter, SIGF_PARENT);
     }
     else
     {
+        Permit();
         // no one is waiting for us, do the clean up
         ObtainSemaphore(&thread_sem);
         memset(inf, 0, sizeof(ThreadInfo));
